 "history": "Founded in Boston in 1965 by Bill Elfers, Dan Gregory and Charlie Waite \u2014 Elfers leaving American Research & Development, the firm Georges Doriot built \u2014 Greylock is one of the oldest venture partnerships still investing. Its own listings date the book from 1967 (Worthington Biochemical, acquired by Millipore in 1975) and carry the New England industrial and healthcare positions that made the first funds: Millipore (1969), Teradyne (1968), Continental Cablevision (acquired by US West for $11.8B in 1997), Stryker (1974), Prime Computer (1974), Neutrogena (1973, acquired by Johnson & Johnson), ROLM (acquired by IBM in 1984) and Genetics Institute (1980). The software era ran through Cullinane/Cullinet (1977, the first software company on the NYSE), McCormack & Dodge (1980), Mentor Graphics (1981), Banyan Systems (1983), Ascend Communications (1989, acquired by Lucent for $20B) and Red Hat (1998, acquired by IBM for $34B \u2014 the largest exit in the book). The firm moved west in stages and is now headquartered at 140 New Montgomery Street in San Francisco with an office on Sand Hill Road. The modern record is built on David Sze's LinkedIn Series B (2004) and Facebook Series B (2006), Reid Hoffman's Airbnb Series A (2010), Asheem Chandna's $250K seed check into Palo Alto Networks (2005) and Aneel Bhusri backing Workday at its 2005 formation while a Greylock partner; more recently Figma (Series A, 2015), Discord (2016), Coinbase (2017), Rubrik, Okta, Cloudera, Docker, Instagram, Roblox, Nextdoor and Cribl, and an AI book that includes Anthropic, OpenAI, Inflection, Abnormal, Snorkel, Adept, Braintrust and Baseten. The firm's Israel practice, Greylock IL, cut its ties in January 2015 and trades as 83North; its funds and companies are outside this base.",
 "investment_concept": "Writes first institutional checks into enterprise infrastructure, security, consumer networks and AI, and follows them for a decade or more. Greylock describes itself as partnering with founders at the idea stage \u2014 Palo Alto Networks and Workday were both built from inside the firm's offices \u2014 and its own portfolio field records the round it entered at rather than the round it led. It runs one fund family rather than sector or stage sleeves, with parallel -A funds and Principals vehicles alongside each numbered flagship.",
